    simple: "The expected value is the average result you would get if you repeated a chance experiment a very large number of times. It weighs each possible outcome by how likely it is.",
    precise: "The expectation of a discrete random variable $X$ is $E[X] = \\sum_x x \\, P(X = x)$. For a continuous one the sum becomes an integral against the density. Expectation is linear: $E[aX + bY] = aE[X] + bE[Y]$, whether or not $X$ and $Y$ are independent. The expected running time of a randomized algorithm is an expectation over its own random choices.",
