    kind: 'idea',
    simple: "Entropy measures how unpredictable a source of messages is. The more surprising the next symbol tends to be, the higher the entropy, and the more space is needed to record it.",
    precise: "The Shannon entropy of a source that emits symbol $x$ with probability $p(x)$ is $H = -\\sum_x p(x) \\log_2 p(x)$ bits per symbol. It is the average information content of a symbol. By Shannon's source coding theorem, for a source emitting independent symbols with these probabilities, it is also the lower limit on the average number of bits per symbol that any lossless code can achieve. Entropy is greatest when all symbols are equally likely.",
    example: "A fair coin has an entropy of 1 bit per toss, while a coin that lands heads 9 times in 10 has about 0.47 bits, so a long record of its tosses can be compressed to less than half its length.",
    caution: "Information entropy is related to the entropy of thermodynamics but is not the same thing. It is also a property of the source's probabilities, not of one particular message.",
